    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "A belt is drawn tightly around three circles of radius $10$ cm each, as shown. The length of the belt, in cm, can be written in the form $a + b\\pi$ for rational numbers $a$ and $b$. What is the value of $a + b$? [asy]\n\nsize(120); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));\npicture p; real r = 3^.5/2;\npair A = expi(-pi/6); draw(p, circle(A, r)); draw(p, (A.x,A.y - r) -- (-A.x,A.y - r));\nadd(p); add(rotate(120)*p); add(rotate(240)*p);\n[/asy]\n\nThink step by step inside <think> </think> tags, then give your final answer inside \\boxed{}."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To determine the length of the belt, we need to analyze the configuration of the belt around three circles of radius \\(10\\) cm each. The belt forms a closed loop that wraps around the circles, touching the circles at some points.\n\nFirst, let's visualize the setup. The centers of the three circles are arranged such that they form an equilateral triangle with each side equal to \\(20\\) cm (since the radius of each circle is \\(10\\) cm, and the distance between the centers of two circles is the sum of their radii, \\(10 + 10 = 20\\) cm).\n\nThe belt wraps around the circles in such a way that it touches the circles at three points, forming a straight segment between the points where the belt touches the circles. This straight segment is the base of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(20\\) cm.\n\nThe length of the belt can be broken down into two parts:\n1. The straight segments that connect the points where the belt touches the circles.\n2. The curved segments that wrap around the circles.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the length of the straight segments\nThe belt forms three straight segments, each of length \\(20\\) cm. Therefore, the total length of the straight segments is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 20 = 60 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the length of the curved segments\nEach curved segment is a quarter-circle because the belt wraps around the circle in a quarter-circle shape. The radius of each quarter-circle is \\(10\\) cm.\n\nThe circumference of a full circle with radius \\(10\\) cm is:\n\\[\n2\\pi \\times 10 = 20\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nSince each curved segment is a quarter-circle, the length of one curved segment is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\times 20\\pi = 5\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThere are three such curved segments, so the total length of the curved segments is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 5\\pi = 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the total length of the belt\nAdding the lengths of the straight segments and the curved segments, we get:\n\\[\n60 + 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the belt can be written in the form \\(a + b\\pi\\) where \\(a = 60\\) and \\(b = 15\\).\n\n### Step 4: Determine the value of \\(a + b\\)\n\\[\na + b = 60 + 15 = 75\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{75}\n\\]"
